; scheduling.lib.pol — a DOMAIN library: the vocabulary of a blocking job shop.
;
; The sibling of queens/chess.lib.pol, and the same split: a library says what
; EXISTS, a model says what may HAPPEN. Both job shop scenarios load this one
; and then differ only in their moves, which is exactly the thing they are a
; pair in order to compare.
;
; WHY IT LIVES HERE, one level up from both. Design D3's first search rule is
; the including file's own directory, so a library serving ONE folder sits in
; it — chess.lib.pol does. This one serves two, `jobshop-possible/` and
; `jobshop-best/`, so it sits at the level that contains both and they load it
; as `(load "../scheduling.lib.pol")`. A relative name resolves against the
; including file's directory, which is the same rule doing the same work.
;
; It pays the namespace, as any library does (§7): every model loading it gives
; up `shop`, `job`, `machine`, `tick`, `idle`, `hand-over` and the rest. That
; cost is why this is a DOMAIN library and not an addition to stdlib — a
; standard library may not spend the shared namespace on a worldly concept.

(load "stdlib.pol")

(schema shop

  ; TIME as a small named scale (Appendix G). A ladder of ticks, walked by
  ; `next`, running off the end into `vacant` — which is what stops a clock
  ; rather than a guard, since a move whose chain has no answer is absent.
  (type tick    (arrow next (to tick) fixed vacatable))
  (type clock-t (arrow at (to tick)))

  (type yn      (no yes))

  ; A MACHINE is a resource at most one job holds. `held-by` vacant is idle;
  ; that the arrow is vacatable at all is what lets a shop be empty.
  (type machine (maybe held-by job))

  (type stage-t (queued on-first on-second done))

  ; A JOB is a routing plus where it has got to. `first` and `second` are
  ; `fixed` — a routing is wiring, decided before the shop opens, not state.
  (type job
    (arrow stage  (to stage-t))
    (arrow moved  (to yn))            ; has it already acted this tick?
    (arrow first  (to machine) fixed)
    (arrow second (to machine) fixed)))

; ── the shop, said in its own words ──────────────────────────────────────────

(form (idle M)       => (not (defined M.held-by)))
(form (at-stage J S) => (is J.stage S))

; Every job finished. B names the binder, because a form template's list head
; must be a slot. Used by the models AND by both .claims files — a question is
; a document of its own (wish 12), but it may share a vocabulary.
(form (all-done B)   => (all (B job) (is B.stage done)))

; ── the BLOCKING discipline, which is the whole subject ──────────────────────
;
; A job seizes the next machine BEFORE releasing the one it is on. That single
; rule is what makes a job shop a job shop, and it is what makes it deadlock:
; three jobs each holding one machine and waiting for the next cannot move, and
; none of them has done anything wrong.
;
; Naming it is worth more than the two lines it saves. `@E` splices whatever
; else the move does, so a timed shop can add its own effects without the rule
; being written twice — the effect list is one datum, which is what lets a form
; produce it (§10.2: splicing inside a list is only `@SLOT` on a `&rest`).
(form (hand-over J FROM TO &rest E)
  => (do (set TO.held-by J) (vacate FROM.held-by) @E))

; ── the TURN discipline: a job acts at most once per tick ────────────────────
;
; A matched guard/effect pair, deliberately named together so a reader meets
; the rule rather than an ad-hoc flag. A shop with no clock simply never uses
; them, and pays nothing for that: a cell that is never written is the same
; cell in every situation.
(form (may-move J)  => (is J.moved no))
(form (took-turn J) => (set J.moved yes))

; The clock advances and every job's turn comes back. `@R` is the resets,
; because the library cannot know which jobs a shop has — and the moment `set`
; took a chain (§10.3), this stopped needing one transition per tick.
(form (tick-over N C &rest R)
  => (transition N (when (is C.at C.at)) (do (set C.at C.at.next) @R)))

; ── the shop floor ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; Three jobs, three machines, and routings that CHASE EACH OTHER: a needs m1
; then m2, b needs m2 then m3, c needs m3 then m1. That cycle is not decoration
; — it is the smallest arrangement in which the blocking rule above can trap
; every job at once, which is what `jobshop-possible` goes on to find.
;
; Nine ticks are available. Whether anything ever moves the clock is the
; model's business, not this file's.
(instance start (of shop)
  (tick t1 t2 t3 t4 t5 t6 t7 t8 t9)
  (clock-t clk)
  (machine m1 m2 m3)
  (job a b c)
  (next (t1 t2) (t2 t3) (t3 t4) (t4 t5) (t5 t6) (t6 t7) (t7 t8) (t8 t9) (t9 vacant))
  (at (clk t1))
  (first  (a m1) (b m2) (c m3))
  (second (a m2) (b m3) (c m1))
  (stage  (a queued) (b queued) (c queued))
  (moved  (a no) (b no) (c no))
  (held-by (m1 vacant) (m2 vacant) (m3 vacant)))
